  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 359 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nRain begins across southwest Minnesota late tonight, aided by upglide\n& increasing warm/moist advection from a strengthening \nsoutherly low-level jet. Showers spread into western & south- \ncentral Minnesota overnight into early Saturday morning, then \nexpand northeast across central Minnesota and the Twin Cities \nthrough the morning into early afternoon. The rain eventually \nreaches western Wisconsin by early afternoon, but should become \nlighter and more scattered as it encounters drier air farther \neast. Light rain will be falling through much of Saturday for \nmost, with the rain tapering off during the afternoon across \nwestern Minnesota & the evening across eastern Minnesota/western\nWisconsin. However, patchy drizzle may still persist into the \nnight as the southerly flow aloft continues into Sunday morning.\nThe heaviest rain should favor western Minnesota where rainfall\namounts of 0.75-1 are expected across a fairly broad area. \nRainfall amounts gradually decrease off to the east with amounts\nonly on the order of a tenth or less across western Wisconsin. \nRain during the day will keep temperatures cool, not warming \nmuch from our overnight lows in the upper 50s. However - peaks \nof afternoon sun across western Minnesota after the rain ends, &\nacross western Wisconsin before the rain begins, will allow \nhighs to warm into the upper 60s. Sunday will be dry but cloud \ncover could persist through the day, likely limiting \ntemperatures to the low to mid 60s. If we get more sunshine \nduring the afternoon, highs near 70 look possible.\n\nOur next chance for widespread rain comes Monday night into \nTuesday as an upper-level disturbance & associated cold front \nmove through the Upper Midwest. Rain develops across western \nMinnesota after midnight Monday night, then spreads across \ncentral & southern Minnesota and western Wisconsin Tuesday \nmorning. The steadiest rain looks to fall during the morning \ninto early afternoon, before gradually ending from west to east \nTuesday afternoon & evening. The timing of the front during the \novernight & morning hours likely prevents any chances for \nwidespread thunderstorms & heavier rainfall totals - most \nlocations should see around 0.25-0.5 of rain but can\u2019t rule out \nsome heavier amounts near an inch if embedded thunderstorms \noccur.\n\nCooler air filters back into the region behind the front Tuesday into\nWednesday, with more fall-like temperatures through the rest of\nthe week with highs in the low to mid 60s & lows in the 40s. \nThe pattern may remain active as another disturbance develops \nalong or north of the stalled frontal zone late Wednesday into \nThursday. Some spread in the guidance exists on just how far\nnorth the precipitation fro this system may extend into\nMinnesota, but at least the far-souther portion of the state\nappears positioned for another round of appreciable rainfall.\nOtherwise, high pressure building over the Upper Midwest should\nkeep us cool & dry through next weekend.",
